  [qr1_x2_over_xplus1] (479 tok) "Let's go through the differentiation step by step to check whether your result is correct.\n\nYou're given the function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{x^2}{x + 1}\n$$\n\nThis is a **quotient**, so we can use the **quotient rule**:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\frac{u}{v} \\right)' = \\frac{u'v - uv'}{v^2}\n$$\n\nLet’s define:\n- $ u = x^2 $, so $ u' = 2x $\n- $ v = x + 1 $, so $ v' = 1 $\n\nNow apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{(2x)(x + 1) - (x^2)(1)}{(x + 1)^2}\n$$\n\nNow simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n(2x)(x + 1) = 2x^2 + 2x\n$$\n$$\n(x^2)(1) = x^2\n$$\n$$\n\\text{So the numerator is: } 2x^2 + 2x - x^2 = x^2 + 2x\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{x^2 + 2x}{(x + 1)^2}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Your answer was:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{2x(x + 1) + x^2}{(x + 1)^2}\n$$\n\nLet’s simplify that to check:\n\n$$\n2x(x + 1) = 2x^2 + 2x\n$$\n$$\n2x^2 + 2x + x^2 = 3x^2 + 2x\n$$\n\nSo your expression simplifies to:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{3x^2 + 2x}{(x + 1)^2}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ❌ So your answer is **not correct**.\n\nThe correct derivative is:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\frac{x^2 + 2x}{(x + 1)^2}\n$$\n\nLet me know if you'd like to see the derivative using the **product rule** as well!"
